    "https://uis.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/1339?c=uis": {
      "title": "Associate Director or Director of Development, CBM",
      "description": "Sign In Create Profile English (US) English (UK) Associate Director or Director of Development, CBM Springfield, IL, United States | 1005203 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/20/2026 Back to Search Associate Director or Director of Development, CBM | Job Details Associate Director or Director of Development, CBM Posting Category: E About UIS: Located in the state capital, the University of Illinois Springfield is one of three members of the University of Illinois system. The University serves more than 4,000 students in 44 graduate and 56 undergraduate programs.  You are encouraged to visit the university web page at http://www.uis.edu . Application deadline: Open Until Filled Annual Salary Range: $73,000 - $83,000 Summary: Representative who plans strategies and actively seeks major gifts for the benefit of the College of Business and Management at UIS. This position supports the College of Business and Management's major gift fundraising program. The position emphasizes identifying, cultivating, soliciting, and stewarding major gift donors and prospects. The individual is expected to develop and manage a portfolio of approximately 40 qualified major gift prospects, a major gift stewardship portfolio, and a major gift referral list, and to have 15 or more face-to-face donor meetings or other significant donor contacts per month. Duties & Responsibilities: Identify, qualify, cultivate, solicit, and steward major gift prospects and donors in collaboration with university leadership, the College of Business and Management, and UIF, with a focus on the College of Business and Management. Major Donor Solicitation: Proactively asks prospects for significant, often College of Business and Management-designated gifts. Identifies, qualifies, cultivates, solicits, and provides stewardship activities for major gift prospects and donors, and serves as prospect manager and solicitor for major donor prospects with potential of $25,000 or more, coordinating all activities associated with securing and stewarding major gift commitments. Travel is necessary to meet with prospects. Prepare high-level funding proposals, donor correspondence, and other written materials. Meets or exceeds individual annual donor and corporation/foundation revenue goals. Serve as prospect manager and solicitor for major donor prospects with the potential of making philanthropic gifts of $25,000 or more, coordinating all activities associated with securing and stewarding major gift commitments. Assist in directing capital and comprehensive campaigns of UIF and the UIS campus. Provide development leadership and plan and execute fundraising initiatives for the College of Business and Management, coordinating with college leadership. Assist with College of Business and Management’s social media when pertaining to Alumni/Donor Spotlights, gift announcements, fundraising efforts, etc. This position is not responsible for creating content but should help build the strategy and guide execution of the approved plan. Track development activity and donor interactions in the UIF fundraising database and collaborate with Prospect Development on portfolio management. Stewardship: Leads, conducts, and evaluates donor recognition. Develops impact statements to demonstrate outcomes of gifts. Responsible for gift acknowledgment and ensuring all donors are appropriately thanked. College Representation: Represents the college at various community and/or business meetings, committees, and task forces; promotes existing and new programs and/or policies. Other Duties as Assigned: Perform other duties as assigned, including enthusiastically participating in college fundraising and engagement activities and adapting to new work situations, people, ideas, procedures, and organizational structures to accommodate an evolving work environment. May include representing the unit at campus fundraising events and supporting UIS Advancement staff. Travel to local and regional territories to develop working relationships with high-end prospective donors; some travel will be in conjunction with the UIS Vice Chancellor for Advancement/UIF Senior Vice President, the Associate Vice Chancellor for Development, the Dean of the College of Business and Management, university leadership, and/or the UIS Chancellor. Assist with and participate in donor/prospect activities, including special events and campus visits. Participate and serve as a liaison to councils, boards, and groups, as requested, to enhance overall development effort. Minimum Qualifications: 1. Bachelor's degree 2. Experience in development, fundraising, donor relations, general management, or marketing. 3. Must maintain confidentiality of donor files and information. 4. Willingness and ability to assume responsibility for high-level projects assigned. Preferred Qualifications: 1. Master's degree 2. Associate Director of Development, three (3) or more years; Director of Development, five (5) or more years of combined professional experience in development, fundraising, or donor relations. 3. Demonstrated professional experience in higher education or other nonprofit environments. Knowledge, Skills & Abilities: Excellent written and oral communication skills, with the ability to adapt their writing style for all manners of communication, including email, formal letters, informal notes, extensive funding proposals, briefings, donor meetings, and presentations. Proficient in computer use, including word processing, data/information base systems, and programs to create presentations. General knowledge of and the ability to communicate the social benefits of public radio. Ability to multitask and prioritize work accordingly, and work independently to meet deadlines. Ability to assume project planning responsibilities, to analyze data and regulations, and to provide exceptional attention to detail. Skill in interpersonal communications and a sincere commitment to work collaboratively with team members and constituents. Travel Requirements: 40% Traveling within the region to meet with donors and funders, attend meetings, and engage with other staff. Employment Policies and other information: The University of Illinois System is an equal opportunity, affirmative action employer dedicated to building a community of excellence, equity, and diversity. We are committed to fostering an inclusive environment and we welcome applications from qualified individuals of all backgrounds and identities. The University of Illinois System does not discriminate against any applicant or employee based on their real or perceived race, color, religion, sex, national origin, ancestry, age, order of protection status, genetic information, marital status, disability, sexual orientation including gender identity, unfavorable discharge from the military or status as a protected veteran and will comply with all federal and state nondiscrimination, equal opportunity and affirmative action laws, orders and regulations. Visit University of Illinois Non-Discrimination Statement The University of Illinois System conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer of employment. Convictions are not a bar to employment. Background checks will be performed in compliance with state and federal law. The University of Illinois System requires candidates selected for hire to disclose any documented finding of sexual misconduct or sexual harassment and to authorize inquiries to current and former employers regarding findings of sexual misconduct or sexual harassment. For more information, visit Policy on Consideration of Sexual Misconduct in Prior Employment . As a qualifying federal contractor, the University of Illinois System uses E-Verify to verify employment eligibility . This position is intended to be eligible for benefits . This includes Health, Dental, Vision, Life Insurance, a Retirement Plan, Paid time Off, and Tuition waivers for employees and dependents. Artificial Intelligence (AI) tools may be used in some portions of the candidate review process for this position, however, all employment decisions will be made by a person. UIS employment in this role is not covered by Social Security, and instead, is covered by the State University Retirement System. You will not pay Social Security taxes and your earning will not be on your Social Security Record. (Your record will, however, show your Medicare wages. Your pension from the State Universities Retirement System may affect the amount of your: Social Security benefit and Social Security benefit as a spouse Applicants with disabilities are encouraged to apply and may request a reasonable accommodation under the Americans with Disabilities Act (2008) to complete the application and/or interview process. Requests may be submitted by contacting UIS HR at hremployment@uis.edu or call 217-206-6652. ***Please Note: All postings close at 5:00pm CST on the posting close date.*** Apply Now Share Save Job Looking for more University of Illinois positions? We invite you to search for positions at each of the University of Illinois' locations, the University of Illinois System Office and the University of Illinois Foundation. The University of Illinois System Office supports the primary missions of teaching, research, public service, and economic development for all of the Universities and offers positions in all locations. The University of Illinois Foundation is the official fundraising and private gift-receiving agency for the University of Illinois and also offers position in all locations.  Click the links below to search for other University of Illinois positions. University of Illinois Chicago University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign University of Illinois System Office University of Illinois Foundation The University of Illinois System is an equal opportunity employer, including but not limited to disability and/or veteran status, and complies with all applicable state and federal employment mandates. Please visit Required Employment Notices and Posters to view our non-discrimination statement and find additional information about required background checks, sexual harassment/misconduct disclosures, COVID-19 vaccination requirement, and employment eligibility review through E-Verify. University of Illinois at Springfield Annual Security and Fire Safety Report The University of Illinois Springfield Annual Security and Fire Safety Report is available. This report includes statistics from the previous three years concerning reported crime that occurred on campus, in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by the University of Illinois Springfield campus and statistics for fires in on-campus student housing. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security and other safety information along with fire safety policies and procedures. To access the report online: http://www.uis.edu/about/overview/safety/ To obtain a hard copy: University Police Department, University of Illinois Springfield, One University Plaza, MS PDB1, Springfield, IL 62703-5407 University of Illinois at Springfield . One University Plaza . MS HRB30 . Springfield, Illinois 62703 . 217-206-6652. www.uis.edu/humanresources"
    },
    "https://uis.csod.com/ux/ats/careersite/1/home/requisition/1349?c=uis": {
      "title": "Associate Professor or Professor and Founding Director, School of Applied Engineering",
      "description": "Sign In Create Profile English (US) English (UK) Associate Professor or Professor and Founding Director, School of Applied Engineering Springfield, IL, United States | 1005220 Apply Now Share Save Job Posted on: 10/29/2026 Back to Search Associate Professor or Professor and Founding Director, School of Applied Engineering | Job Details Associate Professor or Professor and Founding Director, School of Applied Engineering Posting Category: Summary: The University of Illinois Springfield (UIS) invites applications for the Founding Director of its planned School of Applied Engineering. This is a unique opportunity for an entrepreneurial academic leader to build a new School focused on preparing students for engineering and technology careers through an innovative, work-based learning model. The School will launch with a Bachelor of Applied Science in Engineering Technology that integrates academic instruction with meaningful workplace experience. Through partnerships with employers and community colleges, the School aims to create accessible educational pathways that connect students, industry, and the region’s evolving workforce needs. The Founding Director will play a central role in shaping the School’s academic identity, building its faculty and partnerships, and establishing the systems needed for long-term success. The Director will lead curriculum implementation, faculty recruitment, student pathway development, industry engagement, and planning for ABET accreditation. We seek a collaborative, forward-thinking leader who combines academic credibility with practical engineering experience and a commitment to educational innovation. The successful candidate will be comfortable building new initiatives, working across institutional boundaries, and translating an ambitious vision into a sustainable academic enterprise. This is a full-time, on-campus tenure-system appointment at the rank of Associate or Full Professor, with the potential for a tenure waiver subject to university policies and approval. The position carries a nine-month academic appointment with an administrative stipend for year-round leadership responsibilities. Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Duties & Responsibilities: Strategic Leadership and School Development Provide the vision, leadership, and direction needed to establish and grow the School of Applied Engineering. Develop and implement a strategic plan that supports academic quality, enrollment growth, financial sustainability, and industry relevance. Recruit, mentor, and support faculty while building a collaborative and innovative academic culture. Establish the administrative processes, policies, and resources necessary for effective School operations. Serve as the School’s Unit Executive Officer (UEO) and represent the School in College and University leadership activities. Academic Innovation and Student Success Lead the implementation and continuous improvement of the Bachelor of Applied Science in Engineering Technology. Advance a work-based learning model that meaningfully integrates academic preparation with applied experience in industry settings. Work with faculty and partners to ensure curriculum relevance, academic rigor, and alignment with evolving engineering technology practices. Establish assessment and continuous improvement processes that support student success and progress toward ABET accreditation. Develop clear educational pathways for transfer students, working professionals, and other students seeking engineering technology careers. Industry and Educational Partnerships Build and sustain partnerships with employers to support work-based learning, curriculum development, and student career opportunities. Collaborate with community colleges to develop coordinated transfer pathways and expand access to engineering technology education. Engage regional industry, economic development organizations, and other stakeholders to align the School’s work with emerging workforce needs. Identify opportunities for external funding, applied research, and collaborative initiatives that strengthen the School’s mission. Teaching, Scholarship, and Service Teach one to two upper-division Engineering Technology courses annually. Maintain a record of scholarship appropriate to academic rank, with opportunities for contributions in applied engineering, engineering education, industry-engaged research, and work-based learning. Contribute to the University’s academic mission through service, professional engagement, and institutional leadership. Minimum Qualifications: Earned doctorate in engineering, engineering education, engineering technology, or a closely related field; Record of teaching, scholarship, and service sufficient for tenure at the rank of Associate or Full Professor; Significant applied or professional engineering experience appropriate for engineering technology programs; Demonstrated academic or organizational leadership experience (e.g., program director, department chair, center director, or comparable role); Experience with curriculum development and academic assessment; Ability to build and sustain partnerships with employers and external stakeholders. Strong communication, collaboration, and organizational leadership skills. Preferred Qualifications: Experience establishing or significantly expanding an academic program, department, school, or comparable initiative. Experience with work-based learning, cooperative education, apprenticeships, or other forms of industry-integrated education. Experience with ABET accreditation or comparable academic quality assurance processes. Experience developing community college partnerships, transfer pathways, or programs serving nontraditional students. Experience securing external funding, developing industry-supported initiatives, or leading applied research partnerships. Demonstrated success in student recruitment, enrollment growth, or development of new educational markets. Application deadline: 1/4/2027 Anticipated Start Date: July 1, 2027, with an earlier start date negotiable. Compensation and Appointment: The position offers a nine-month base salary of $135,000–$165,000, commensurate with qualifications and experience, plus an administrative stipend for year-round Director responsibilities. This is a full-time, on-campus tenure-system academic appointment. UIS tenure-system faculty are expected to be available and engaged in the university community, with assigned professional responsibilities for teaching, scholarship, and service. Application instructions: Click the \"Apply Now\" button at the top or bottom of the posting, create or update your profile, complete the \nonline application, and upload current curriculum vita or resume, cover letter addressing the required and preferred qualifications, leadership philosophy, and interest in this founding role; and a concept paper (2–3 pages): the candidate’s approach to launching the BAS in Engineering Technology through a work-based learning model at a public regional university, addressing program design, the ABET ETAC pathway, employer and community college partnerships, and enrollment. Contact information for professional references will be requested later in the search process. Formal review \nof applications will begin soon after application deadline and continue until the position is filled or the search is terminated. About\nUIS: Located in the state capital, the University of Illinois Springfield is one of three members of the University of Illinois system. The University serves approximately 4,000 students in 60 undergraduate \nprograms of study, 41 minors, 44 graduate programs of study, 1 doctorate, 43 graduate certificates, coursework that leads to 8 ISBE endorsements. The academic curriculum offers students a strong liberal \narts core, a wide range of professional programs, and close faculty interaction. Making the most of its location in the state capital, UIS is broadly engaged in public policy and service. Its diverse \nstudent body includes traditional, non-traditional, and international students. Twenty-five percent of majors are in 17 undergraduate and graduate online degree programs and the campus has received \nseveral national awards for its implementation of online learning. UIS faculty are committed teachers, active scholars, and professionals in service to society. The University emphasizes excellence in \nteaching, and faculty are also expected to provide a balance of scholarship and service activities to meet requirements for tenure and promotion. You are encouraged to visit the university web page at https://www.uis.edu and the college web page at https://uis.edu/chst About Springfield, Illinois: https://www.uis.edu/academic-affairs/about-our-springfield-community/ The University of Illinois System is an equal opportunity, affirmative action employer dedicated to building a community of excellence, equity, and diversity. We are committed to fostering an inclusive environment and we welcome applications from qualified individuals of all backgrounds and identities. The University of Illinois System does not discriminate against any applicant or employee based on their real or perceived race, color, religion, sex, national origin, ancestry, age, order of protection status, genetic information, marital status, disability, sexual orientation including gender identity, unfavorable discharge from the military or status as a protected veteran and will comply with all federal and state nondiscrimination, equal opportunity and affirmative action laws, orders and regulations. Visit University of Illinois Non-Discrimination Statement The University of Illinois System conducts background checks on all job candidates upon acceptance of a contingent offer of employment. Convictions are not a bar to employment. Background checks will be performed in compliance with state and federal law. The University of Illinois System requires candidates selected for hire to disclose any documented finding of sexual misconduct or sexual harassment and to authorize inquiries to current and former employers regarding findings of sexual misconduct or sexual harassment. For more information, visit Policy on Consideration of Sexual Misconduct in Prior Employment . As a qualifying federal contractor, the University of Illinois System uses E-Verify to verify employment eligibility . The University of Illinois offers a very competitive benefits portfolio. Click for a complete list of Employee Benefits. Artificial Intelligence (AI) tools may be used in some portions of the candidate review process for this position, however, all employment decisions will be made by a person. UIS employment in this role is not covered by Social Security, and instead, is covered by the State Universities Retirement System You do not pay Social Security taxes and your earnings will not be on your Social Security record. (Your record will, however, show your Medicare wages.) Your pension from the State Universities Retirement System may affect the amount of your: Social Security benefit and Social Security benefit as a spouse Applicants with disabilities are encouraged to apply and may request a reasonable accommodation under the Americans with Disabilities Act (2008) to complete the application and/or interview process. Requests may be submitted by contacting UIS HR at hremployment@uis.edu or call 217-206-6652. ***Please Note: All postings close at 5:00pm CST on the posting close date.*** Apply Now Share Save Job Looking for more University of Illinois positions? We invite you to search for positions at each of the University of Illinois' locations, the University of Illinois System Office and the University of Illinois Foundation. The University of Illinois System Office supports the primary missions of teaching, research, public service, and economic development for all of the Universities and offers positions in all locations. The University of Illinois Foundation is the official fundraising and private gift-receiving agency for the University of Illinois and also offers position in all locations.  Click the links below to search for other University of Illinois positions. University of Illinois Chicago University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign University of Illinois System Office University of Illinois Foundation The University of Illinois System is an equal opportunity employer, including but not limited to disability and/or veteran status, and complies with all applicable state and federal employment mandates. Please visit Required Employment Notices and Posters to view our non-discrimination statement and find additional information about required background checks, sexual harassment/misconduct disclosures, COVID-19 vaccination requirement, and employment eligibility review through E-Verify. University of Illinois at Springfield Annual Security and Fire Safety Report The University of Illinois Springfield Annual Security and Fire Safety Report is available. This report includes statistics from the previous three years concerning reported crime that occurred on campus, in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by the University of Illinois Springfield campus and statistics for fires in on-campus student housing. The report also includes institutional policies concerning campus security and other safety information along with fire safety policies and procedures. To access the report online: http://www.uis.edu/about/overview/safety/ To obtain a hard copy: University Police Department, University of Illinois Springfield, One University Plaza, MS PDB1, Springfield, IL 62703-5407 University of Illinois at Springfield . One University Plaza . MS HRB30 . Springfield, Illinois 62703 . 217-206-6652. www.uis.edu/humanresources"
